\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{Spending more output tokens on a task predicts failing it, in each of the fifteen tiers we compute it for.} Point-biserial correlation between per-task output tokens and task success within each tier on TerminalBench V2.1, over single-run records from campaigns that predate the leaderboard protocol (\S\ref{sec:measure}), not the pooled multi-run accuracies used elsewhere. All fifteen coefficients are negative (Fisher $z$ pooled mean $-0.281$, range $-0.482$ to $-0.100$). Weak models being verbose does not explain it: the two most negative tiers sit mid-pack on accuracy, and the least negative, Gemini~3.7~Flash, among the most accurate. Providers meter output tokens, so a buyer is billed for the quantity that anti-predicts the outcome they want.}
\label{tab:tokencorr}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Accuracy} & \textbf{$n$} & \textbf{Mean output tokens} & \textbf{$r_{pb}$}  \\
\midrule
\TOKENCORRROWS
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{\textbf{A single serving flag moves a local or on-prem tier's accuracy by \ServSpanLo\ to \ServSpanHi\ points, and the failure is indistinguishable from a capability limit.} Each row is the same model, benchmark, harness, and hardware under two serving configurations that differ in one flag; the cells come from these serving-flag runs, not from each tier's accuracy of record (Figure~\ref{fig:ipd-ipj}). GLM-5.2 emits DeepSeek-R1-format reasoning the harness cannot parse without the matching flag, so about 44\% of its turns become error text. Qwen3.6-27B emits tool calls as plain text that Codex CLI cannot see as structured calls, so the agent exits after about one call per task; Terminus~2 parses text itself and is unaffected. DeepSeek-V4-Flash overflowed a context window we had capped at a fraction of what the model supports, and the overflows were logged as timeouts; its corrected cell is the mean of the three complete runs at the raised context length over all \NTASKS\ tasks, a fourth, interrupted run excluded. That flag moves the tier by \ServSpanDS\ points, far less than the other two, so the size of the effect is a property of the flag rather than of misconfiguration in general.}
\label{tab:serving}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.28\textwidth}rr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Tier} & \textbf{Harness} & \textbf{Serving flag} & \textbf{Misconfigured} & \textbf{Corrected}  \\
\midrule
GLM-5.2 & Terminus~2 & \texttt{-{}-reasoning-parser deepseek\_r1} & \ServMisGLM\% & \ServCorrGLM\% \\
Qwen3.6-27B & Codex CLI & vLLM tool-call parser & \ServMisQwen\% & \ServCorrQwen\% \\
DeepSeek-V4-Flash & Terminus~2 & \texttt{-{}-max-model-len} 64K $\rightarrow$ 128K & \ServMisDS\% & \ServCorrDS\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
